 "beats": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "He was one of the most famous men in the world. Then America took away his passport.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "p1942",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.22
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   },
   "section": "hook",
   "overlays": {
    "hook": "One of the most famous men alive. *Then America took his passport.*"
   },
   "captions": false,
   "speed": 1.3
  },
  {
   "id": "name",
   "text": "His name was Paul Robeson.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Paul Robeson",
    "sub": "singer, actor, athlete, activist"
   },
   "section": "who"
  },
  {
   "id": "born",
   "text": "He was born in Princeton, New Jersey, in 1898. His father had escaped slavery and became a minister. When Paul was six, his mother died in a fire.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "senior",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "pin": {
     "label": "Princeton, New Jersey"
    }
   },
   "section": "story"
  },
  {
   "id": "rutgers",
   "text": "He won a scholarship to Rutgers, where he was the only Black student at the time.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "capskull",
    "caption": "Robeson with Cap and Skull inductees, Rutgers, 1919"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1915"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "tryouts",
   "text": "At football tryouts, his own teammates broke his nose and dislocated his shoulder. He made the team anyway.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "A broken nose",
    "sub": "and a dislocated shoulder"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "star",
   "text": "He became an All-American twice, joined Phi Beta Kappa, and graduated as class valedictorian.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "senior",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.3,
     1.45
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "law",
   "text": "Then he earned a law degree from Columbia and joined a New York law firm.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "suit",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "secretary",
   "text": "But a white secretary refused to take dictation from him. So he quit. Not just the job, but the law.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "She refused",
    "sub": "to take his dictation"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "stage",
   "text": "He turned to the stage, and to film.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pulpit",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   },
   "section": "fame"
  },
  {
   "id": "voice",
   "text": "His deep voice made him a star. In Show Boat he sang Ol' Man River, and it became his song.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "sofa",
    "focus": [
     0.33,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "othello",
   "text": "In 1943 he played Othello on Broadway. It ran 296 performances, a Broadway record for Shakespeare.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "othello",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1943"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "movies",
   "text": "He starred in films in America and Britain, in leading roles.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "tux",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "fight",
   "text": "And he used his fame. He sang for workers, and spoke out against lynching and segregation.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "shipyard",
    "caption": "Robeson leads shipyard workers in song, Oakland, 1942"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "soviet",
   "text": "He also praised the Soviet Union. In Cold War America, that made him a target.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "nara",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   },
   "section": "target"
  },
  {
   "id": "peekskill",
   "text": "In 1949, rioters shut down his concert in Peekskill, New York.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Peekskill, 1949",
    "sub": "the concert was shut down"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "passport",
   "text": "In 1950, the State Department took his passport. He could no longer perform abroad.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Passport cancelled",
    "sub": "U.S. State Department, 1950"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1950"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "blacklist",
   "text": "At home he was blacklisted. His records and films were pulled. His income fell from over a hundred thousand dollars a year to almost nothing.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "close",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "border",
   "text": "In 1952, he was even turned back at the Canadian border, though no passport was needed to enter Canada.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Turned back",
    "sub": "U.S.-Canada border, 1952"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "peacearch",
   "text": "So the union that invited him set up a concert at the Peace Arch. He stood on a flatbed truck on the American side and sang across the border to tens of thousands of people.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "bham",
    "caption": "Paul Robeson in concert"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "May 18, 1952"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "huac",
   "text": "In 1956, Congress called him in. Asked why he didn't just stay in Russia, he answered:",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "p1930s",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   },
   "section": "hearing",
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "June 12, 1956"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "father",
   "text": "Because my father was a slave, and my people died to build this country, and I am going to stay here, and have a part of it, just like you.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "\"My father was a slave\"",
    "sub": "Paul Robeson to Congress, 1956"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "unamerican",
   "text": "Then he told them: You are the un-Americans, and you ought to be ashamed of yourselves.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "boss",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "returned",
   "text": "In 1958, after a Supreme Court ruling, he finally got his passport back.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "ejstill",
    "caption": "Paul Robeson, The Emperor Jones, 1933"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1958"
   },
   "section": "end"
  },
  {
   "id": "decline",
   "text": "But his health broke down, and his career never recovered. He died in Philadelphia in 1976. He was 77.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "p1942",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.3,
     1.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "hof",
   "text": "In 1995, he was finally named to the College Football Hall of Fame.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "1995",
    "sub": "College Football Hall of Fame"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "close",
   "text": "That was Paul Robeson.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "shoes",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Follow The Black History Room for more.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "endcard"
   },
   "section": "cta",
   "captions": false
  }
 ],
